<p># When AI Becomes a Colleague: Deep Takeaways from WAIC 2026</p>

<blockquote>July 17-20, 2026, Shanghai — the 9th World AI Conference (WAIC) is about to open. This year's theme: "AI Partners, Co-Creating the Future." AI's role is shifting from "tool" to "collaborator." This article decodes the key signals from five dimensions.</blockquote>

<hr />

<h2>1. From "Shared Planet" to "AI Partners": The Deeper Meaning Behind One Word</h2>

<p>In July 2025, WAIC's theme was "AI Era, Shared Planet" — focused on LLM breakthroughs and global governance. One year later, the theme has upgraded to "AI Partners, Co-Creating the Future." This isn't rhetorical tweaking; it marks a fundamental shift from "technology competition" to "value symbiosis."</p>

<p>Sun Xiaobo, the Foreign Ministry's AI affairs coordinator, elaborated on "partners" at the July 7 press conference: on one hand, AI empowers industries and enters homes as a key helper in work and life; on the other, global AI governance requires nations to build global partnerships.[1]</p>

<p>This "two-way partner" positioning aligns perfectly with industry changes since 2025:</p>

<p>First, AI's capability form has changed — from Q&A mode to autonomous agents that decompose tasks, call tools, and execute closed loops. Second, the human-AI interaction paradigm has shifted — from keyboard commands to voice dialogue to intent sensing; AI now understands context, remembers preferences, and proactively initiates collaboration. Third, organizational forms have transformed — when AI can act as a "digital employee," one person plus AI can form a fully operational company.[2]</p>

<hr />

<h2>2. Four "Firsts" Revealing Structural Shifts in AI Industry</h2>

<h3>2.1 First Three-Site, Four-Venue Layout — Scale Signals Industry Maturity</h3>

<p>WAIC 2026 breaks from a single venue, opening simultaneously across three sites and four venues: Expo, Zhangjiang, and West Bund. Exhibition area exceeds 100,000 sqm for the first time, with 1,100+ exhibitors and 3,000+ products, over 300 of which are global debuts.[1]</p>

<p>This scale isn't just industry enthusiasm — it signals AI has moved from "concept showcases" to "productized deployment." The four themed venues each serve a purpose: H1 focuses on AI applications and ecosystem, H2 on computing infrastructure, H3 on embodied intelligence, and H4 on startups and venture capital.[3] From chips, computing power, models to applications, terminals, and ecosystems — the entire industry chain is presented in one physical space for the first time.</p>

<p>Huawei will showcase the industry's largest supernode — the Atlas 950 SuperPoD live. With 8,192 Ascend computing cards interconnected at high speed and over 500,000-card computing scale, training performance is 17x that of Atlas 900.[4] The accelerated breakthrough of domestic computing power provides critical infrastructure for China's AI industry.</p>

<h3>2.2 First WAIC Academic — Bridging Industry and Academia</h3>

<p>WAIC Academic, China's independently established world-class AI academic conference, is chaired by Turing Award winner Andrew Yao, with Richard Sutton, "father of reinforcement learning," as international co-chair, and Chinese Academy members leading the academic committee. It has received 284 valid submissions from 11 countries; accepted papers will be published by Springer, and several tech companies will factor paper acceptance into campus recruitment evaluations.[1][5]</p>

<p>The significance: WAIC is upgrading from an "industry expo" to a dual-engine "industry + academia" platform. Academic research is no longer peripheral to industry narratives but embedded at the ecosystem's core.</p>

<h3>2.3 First OPC (One-Person Company) Competition — AI's New Organizational Form</h3>

<p>The OPC Pioneer Challenge may be the conference's most forward-looking initiative.</p>

<p>By the numbers: 22 top projects were selected from 711 entries across 8 national competition zones; the first OPC exclusive zone hosts 180 enterprises; WAIC Future Tech screened nearly 180 quality startups from 1,200+ global projects, with over 70% founded less than 3 years ago and more than 50% of founders born after 1990.[1][6]</p>

<p>Why did the OPC model leap from "fringe concept" to "core exhibit" in one conference? Three driving forces:</p>

<p><strong>AI tool capability leap.</strong> An AI agent can now handle the daily task volume of 3-5 full-time employees at less than one-tenth the cost of traditional labor. Open-source models have dramatically lowered AI costs, making them affordable for everyday entrepreneurs.[7]</p>

<p><strong>Full policy support.</strong> National AI industrial policies explicitly support the OPC model. Shanghai offers "pay-after-use" computing policies, plus "3 million" support packages in computing power, tokens, and training data, and up to 100% rent reduction.[1]</p>

<p><strong>Accelerated capital inflow.</strong> The conference brings together 80+ investment institutions including Sequoia, Hillhouse, ZhenFund, SoftBank China, and CICC, with 200 professional investors in dedicated matchmaking zones.[3]</p>

<p>In traditional thinking, entrepreneurship = team + capital + business plan. WAIC 2026's OPC signal rewrites the formula: creativity + AI tool precision = new entrepreneurial entity.</p>

<h3>2.4 First Youth Talent Program with 1,030 Participants — AI-Native Generational Shift</h3>

<p>1,030 AI-talented youth and teens (under 35) from China and abroad will engage with top experts through "debate sessions," "youth-mentor dialogues," and "Developer Night."[1]</p>

<p>This is no mere "student participation." The conference has built a complete education-to-incubation pipeline:</p>

<ul>

<li><strong>Competition layer</strong>: SAIL Award, Youth Excellent Paper Award, Cloud Sail Award, OPC Challenge, and the youth AI music show "Music Creation Universe" — covering everything from top research to public outreach</li>

<li><strong>Academic layer</strong>: WAIC Academic conference with a strict 20% acceptance rate, Springer publication</li>

<li><strong>Incubation layer</strong>: Shanghai Innovation Institute's "Research-Innovation-Learning" ecosystem, AI4S "100 Groups, 100 Projects" special fund of 500 million yuan[1]</li>

<li><strong>Infrastructure layer</strong>: 14 Shanghai universities with AI research institutes, 19 with AI majors, and nearly 300,000 AI professionals citywide — roughly one-third of China's total[1]</li>

</ul>

<p>The system's core goal is not "programming skills" but AI-era core competencies: critical thinking, human-AI collaboration, and AI ethics awareness. Special events like "When AI Meets Intangible Cultural Heritage" and the "AIGC Music Challenge" let youth understand AI through the intersection of humanities and technology.[2]</p>

<hr />

<h2>3. Five Ecosystem Matrices: From "Annual Event" to "Year-Round Ecosystem Network"</h2>

<p>WAIC 2026's newly launched ecosystem matrix may be its most valuable long-term initiative:</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Ecosystem</td><td>Positioning</td><td>Coverage</td></tr>
<tr><td>WAIC Future Tech</td><td>Venture ecosystem</td><td>Startup incubation, investor matching</td></tr>
<tr><td>WAIC CONNECT</td><td>Link ecosystem</td><td>Industry matching, supply chain collaboration</td></tr>
<tr><td>WAIC UP!</td><td>Knowledge ecosystem</td><td>Public knowledge empowerment</td></tr>
<tr><td>WAIC YOUNG</td><td>Youth ecosystem</td><td>Youth innovation talent development</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI GRAVITY</td><td>Go-global ecosystem</td><td>Internationalization of Chinese AI products</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Alongside this, the Hi WAIC APP — an AI-native platform built on multi-agent architecture integrating smart events, supply-demand matching, vertical content, social networking, think tank data, and tiered memberships — launches for the first time.[1] MCP protocol support for personal agent integration is planned for later release.</p>

<p>This marks WAIC's transformation from an annual industry gathering into a year-round "AI super-ecosystem network" — industry matching, capital coordination, and knowledge curation are no longer confined to four days in July.</p>

<hr />

<h2>4. Terminal Explosion: AI Moves from Screen to Physical World</h2>

<p>This year's conference features unprecedented terminal product density.</p>

<p>StepFun launched the LLM-native agent phone STEPX Neo on July 13, powered by the Step AOS operating system and built-in personal agent "Step Amoo" — a "model, software, hardware" trinity. It won WAIC 2026's "Treasure of the Exhibition" award.[8]</p>

<p>Nubia announced it will exhibit the world's first mass-produced AI agent phone at the conference. Ni Fei described the direction as "from feature stacking to native agent" — instead of users adapting to phone interaction logic, the phone actively understands user needs, achieving "listening, working, remembering, and staying secure."[9]</p>

<p>In embodied intelligence, Hall H3 displays 300+ real robots. Unitree will exhibit the world's first manned transformable mecha GD01, switching between humanoid and quadruped modes. Zhiyuan Robotics showcases the Expedition A3 Ultra with autonomous charging and unmanned maintenance for 24/7 operation.[3]</p>

<p>From smartphones to humanoid robots, AI is extending from the "digital brain" to the "physical body."</p>

<hr />

<h2>5. AI Governance: Trust as the Foundation of Human-AI Partnership</h2>

<p>WAIC 2026 also hosts the "High-Level Meeting on Global AI Governance." Based on public information, governance progress at this year's conference includes:[1][10]</p>

<ul>

<li>Continued implementation of the Shanghai Declaration on AI Global Governance and its action plan</li>

<li>MIIT's release of the "AI Ethics Review and Service Measures (Trial)" — pushing ethical governance toward institutionalization, engineering, and standardization</li>

<li>BRICS Partnership Center to release the "International AI Ethics Governance Action Plan"</li>

<li>Official release of the "Shanghai AI Governance Initiative 2026" and "Global AI Governance White Paper 2026"</li>

<li>First dedicated AI Safety exhibition zone</li>

</ul>

<p>These actions, echoing international frameworks like the EU AI Act, point to a shared trend: as AI moves from "tool" to "partner," trust is not a soft "nice-to-have" but a "must-have" determining whether AI can be integrated at scale.</p>

<p>Nine Turing and Nobel laureates will gather at the conference. Richard Sutton delivers a keynote; Yoshua Bengio introduces the UN's AI governance framework.[1] These signals show AI governance moving from "intergovernmental negotiation" toward multi-stakeholder co-governance involving scientists, governments, enterprises, and the public.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Conclusion: Three Core Competencies for the Human-AI Collaboration Era</h2>

<p>WAIC 2026 carries immense signal density. But distilled to the most valuable question — "What should I do?" — three keywords emerge:</p>

<p><strong>AI Collaboration Competency.</strong> This isn't just "knowing how to use AI assistants." It's turning vague needs into clear AI-executable tasks, knowing what to delegate and what to keep, and calibrating collaboration quality through continuous feedback. It's essentially "digital leadership" — you're not managing people, but hybrid human-AI teams.</p>

<p><strong>Value Anchoring.</strong> When AI handles execution, the human value anchor shifts upward — judgment, creativity, empathy. AI provides options; humans make decisions. AI solves "how"; humans define "what."</p>

<p><strong>Continuous Adaptability.</strong> Eight years of WAIC evolution, OPC from nothing to something, agent phones from concept to mass production — any static skill becomes obsolete. The only sustainable competitive advantage is adaptability and human-AI collaboration fluency.</p>

<p>On July 17, 2026, AI officially enters the "colleague era." The question isn't whether it will, but how fluent your collaboration has become.</p>

